From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 01:53:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(llama-cpp): the SYCL pool's refusal reaches the graph
 instead of ending the backend

Carried as a llama.cpp patch beside the others
(patches/0018-sycl-pool-refusal-is-not-a-std-exception.patch), after
0014-sycl-pool-refusal-fails-the-graph.patch, which it completes.

0014 meant a device-memory refusal during a request to fail that request
and leave the backend running. It threw the refusal as a std::runtime_error
and caught it around the graph - but the SYCL backend calls its ops under
its own macros, SYCL_CHECK(CHECK_TRY_ERROR(op(...))), and CHECK_TRY_ERROR
catches any std::exception and aborts. The op that had asked for the
memory took the refusal, and the backend process ended exactly as before
0014, with one more line of log in front. LocalAI answered 500 "error
reading from server: EOF" and loaded the model again.

Seen with the test switch of 0016 on an Intel Arc Pro B70 (a reranker,
GGML_SYCL_POOL_REFUSE=2): three backend processes, each gone at its first
refusal, "SYCL error: CHECK_TRY_ERROR(op(...))" in ggml_sycl_op_mul_mat,
never "the graph is abandoned".

The refusal is now a plain struct, neither a sycl::exception nor a
std::exception, with a static_assert for each: only the graph's own catch
can take it. The request is then answered "Compute error." by the same
process, and the next one is served.

Checked without a device: the backend's real macros under icpx 2025.3.3
abort on the old type and pass the new one; a CPU stand-in that fails two
graphs with GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED gets an error for the request, the
same process, and the same vectors bit for bit at the next request. Its
test on a device is 0016's: the first two requests that grow the pool
answered with an error by the same process, the third served with the
scores of a run without the switch.

Assisted-by: Claude Code
---
 ...-pool-refusal-is-not-a-std-exception.patch | 100 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 100 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0018-sycl-pool-refusal-is-not-a-std-exception.patch

diff --git a/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0018-sycl-pool-refusal-is-not-a-std-exception.patch b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0018-sycl-pool-refusal-is-not-a-std-exception.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..cc1c816
--- /dev/null
+++ b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/patches/0018-sycl-pool-refusal-is-not-a-std-exception.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+sycl: the pool's refusal is not a std::exception, so that it reaches the graph
+
+"sycl: a pool commit the device refuses fails the graph, not the process"
+threw the refusal as a type derived from std::runtime_error and caught it
+around the graph. It never arrived there. The ops are called under the
+backend's own macros,
+
+  SYCL_CHECK(CHECK_TRY_ERROR(op(ctx, src0, src1, dst, ...)));
+
+and CHECK_TRY_ERROR (common.hpp) catches ANY std::exception, prints it and
+returns an error, which SYCL_CHECK turns into an abort. So the op that had
+asked the pool for memory took the refusal and the process ended as it
+always had, with one more line in front:
+
+  E alloc: the device refused 6 MiB for the pool (2 MiB committed): ...
+  the SYCL pool could not commit device memory
+  Exception caught at file:.../ggml-sycl.cpp, line:4221, func:operator()
+  SYCL error: CHECK_TRY_ERROR(op(ctx, src0, src1, dst, src0_dd_i, ...)): Exception caught in this line of code.
+    in function ggml_sycl_op_mul_mat at .../ggml-sycl.cpp:4221
+
+(an Intel Arc Pro B70, a reranker, GGML_SYCL_POOL_REFUSE=2: three
+processes, each gone at its first refusal; no "the graph is abandoned").
+
+The refusal is now a plain struct: neither a sycl::exception, which the
+ops' function-try-blocks end the process on, nor a std::exception, which
+CHECK_TRY_ERROR takes. Two static_asserts hold both. No handler between an
+op and ggml_sycl_graph_compute_or_fail() catches (...): the four in the
+backend are in the host allocator, the multi-device exchange and its
+teardown.
+
+What an abandoned op leaves: its temporaries are the pool's, released in
+reverse order as the stack unwinds; the one thing ops change for good, the
+layout of quantized weights, takes its scratch memory from the device
+allocator, not from the pool, and has its own fallback.
+
+Checked without a device: with the backend's real macros under icpx
+2025.3.3, the old type aborts with the lines above and the new one reaches
+a catch outside the op; and with a CPU stand-in that fails two graphs with
+GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED, llama-server answers "Compute error." (or, with
+a context made for the request, its canary's refusal), keeps its process,
+and serves the next request with the same vectors, bit for bit. On a
+device: GGML_SYCL_POOL_REFUSE=2, the first two requests that grow the pool
+answered with an error by the same process, the third served.
+---
+diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/ggml-sycl.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/ggml-sycl.cpp
+index a9599ca..1aa0170 100644
+--- a/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/ggml-sycl.cpp
++++ b/ggml/src/ggml-sycl/ggml-sycl.cpp
+@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
+ #include <cmath>
+ #include <cstring>
+ #include <stdexcept>
++#include <type_traits>
+ #include <iostream>
+ #include <fstream>
+ #include <stdio.h>
+@@ -2264,11 +2265,21 @@ ggml_backend_buffer_type_t ggml_backend_sycl_host_buffer_type() {
+ // The device refused memory for an op's temporaries. Thrown by the pool and caught by
+ // ggml_backend_sycl_graph_compute(), which fails the GRAPH with GGML_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED: on a card shared with
+ // other models a refusal is contention, not a fault, and the process - with every other model's work and this
+-// model's weights - must outlive it. Not a sycl::exception on purpose: the function-try-blocks that end the
+-// process on one must not see it.
+-struct ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory : public std::runtime_error {
+-    using std::runtime_error::runtime_error;
++// model's weights - must outlive it.
++//
++// NOT a sycl::exception and NOT a std::exception, on purpose. Between the pool and the graph's caller stand the ops,
++// and they end the process on either: their function-try-blocks on a sycl::exception, and CHECK_TRY_ERROR
++// (common.hpp) on ANY std::exception - it catches one, prints it, and SYCL_CHECK aborts. As a std::runtime_error,
++// which this was at first, the refusal never reached the graph: the op that had asked for the memory took it
++// (SYCL_CHECK(CHECK_TRY_ERROR(op(...))) in ggml_sycl_op_mul_mat) and the process ended as it always had. No handler
++// between an op and ggml_sycl_graph_compute_or_fail() catches (...).
++struct ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory {
++    const char * what;
+ };
++static_assert(!std::is_base_of<std::exception, ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory>::value,
++              "CHECK_TRY_ERROR catches std::exception: the pool's refusal must not be one, or it ends the process");
++static_assert(!std::is_base_of<sycl::exception, ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory>::value,
++              "the ops' function-try-blocks end the process on a sycl::exception: the pool's refusal must not be one");
+ 
+ struct ggml_sycl_pool_leg : public ggml_sycl_pool {
+     static const int MAX_SYCL_BUFFERS = 256;
+@@ -2484,7 +2495,7 @@ struct ggml_sycl_pool_vmm : public ggml_sycl_pool {
+                 // nothing of the pool has changed yet: this graph fails, and a later one may ask again
+                 GGML_LOG_ERROR("%s: the device refused %zu MiB for the pool (%zu MiB committed): %s\n", __func__,
+                                reserve_size / (1024 * 1024), pool_size / (1024 * 1024), e.what());
+-                throw ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory("the SYCL pool could not commit device memory");
++                throw ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory{ "the SYCL pool could not commit device memory" };
+             }
+ 
+             // reserve virtual address space (if not already reserved)
+@@ -7393,7 +7404,7 @@ static ggml_status ggml_sycl_graph_compute_or_fail(ggml_backend_sycl_context * s
+     try {
+         ggml_backend_sycl_graph_compute_impl(sycl_ctx, cgraph);
+     } catch (const ggml_sycl_out_of_device_memory & e) {
+-        GGML_LOG_ERROR("%s: %s: the graph is abandoned\n", __func__, e.what());
++        GGML_LOG_ERROR("%s: %s: the graph is abandoned\n", __func__, e.what);
+         try {
+             sycl_ctx->stream()->wait();   // what was submitted before the refusal finishes on memory still held
+         } catch (const sycl::exception &) {
-- 
